course: dictionary
lesson: 1550
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The dictionary lists them as 1) and 2). To pick the right one, read the context, which means the words around it. «A chameleon is a kind of lizard that can change the colour of its skin.» Skin and lizard tell you this is the animal.
explain: The second meaning is figurative, which means it is used like a picture, not for the real thing. If someone says, «Sam is a chameleon: he is cheeky with his mates and polite to the teacher», they are not saying Sam has scaly skin. The clue is that Sam changes how he behaves depending on who he is with.
explain: Ask yourself: is the sentence about a real animal, or about a person's behaviour? Try the literal meaning in the sentence. If it sounds silly, you need the figurative meaning.
fact: A chameleon is a kind of lizard that can change the colour of its skin.
fact: Each chameleon eye moves on its own, so it can look forward and backward at once.
fact: A chameleon catches insects with a very long, sticky tongue, sometimes longer than its body.
fact: Chameleons change colour mostly to show their mood and control their temperature, not mainly to hide.
fact: Figurative meaning: a person who changes their opinions or behaviour to fit in with whoever they are with is also called a chameleon.
exercise: «A chameleon is a four-legged lizard.» What kind of animal is a chameleon? | A bird | A fish | An insect | *A lizard
exercise: «Each chameleon eye moves on its own, so it can look forward and backward at once.» What can a chameleon do with its eyes? | Close both eyes at night | See only colours | Shine in the dark | *Look in two directions at once
exercise: Sam is a chameleon: he is cheeky with his mates and polite to the teacher. What does chameleon mean here? | A quiet, shy child | *Someone who changes behaviour to fit in with whoever they are with | A lizard that changes skin colour | Someone who never moves
test: «Chameleons change colour mostly to show mood and control temperature, not to hide.» Why do chameleons mostly change colour? | To make their tongue longer | To hide from predators | To look like a cartoon | *To show mood and control temperature
test: Priya changes her opinions every time she talks to a new friend, so her teacher calls her a chameleon. What does chameleon mean here? | A kind of lizard | *A person who changes to fit in with whoever they are with | A bird that sings | A type of dance
exam: Which sentence uses chameleon in its first, literal meaning? | Dad is a chameleon at parties, chatting to everyone | *The chameleon on the branch turned from green to brown | Jo is a chameleon: she agrees with whoever is talking | Our class chameleon can never decide what to wear
